'''Metaphysics''' is a branch of philosophy investigating fundamental questions about what exists and the nature and structure of reality. Topics include identity, causation, time, possibility, properties and relations.

== History and scope ==
The term is historically associated with Aristotle's writings collected under the title ''Metaphysics'', including inquiry into being and first principles. Its scope expanded and changed across subsequent philosophical traditions. Ontology concerns what entities and categories exist; modern metaphysics also investigates modality, persistence, causation, grounding, composition and time. No universally accepted demarcation separates all metaphysical questions from every other philosophical field.

== Central problems ==
Ontology asks whether a theory commits us to objects, properties, relations or abstract entities. Identity and persistence concern conditions under which an entity remains the same through change. Modal metaphysics concerns necessity and possibility; logical consistency, physical possibility and metaphysical possibility are distinct notions. The metaphysics of causation investigates what causal relations are, whereas empirical causal inquiry investigates particular effects and mechanisms. Grounding concerns proposed noncausal dependence or explanation; its nature and explanatory role remain contested.

== Methods and epistemic boundaries ==
Arguments can be evaluated for validity, premise plausibility, explanatory power, coherence with other commitments and compatibility with relevant evidence. Formal logic may make a proposed theory explicit but cannot establish the truth of its premises by notation alone. Thought experiments can expose conceptual tensions without independently demonstrating physical possibilities. Scientific theories constrain some metaphysical accounts, while empirical underdetermination may leave competing interpretations open. Distinguish the question of what exists from the epistemological question of what justifies belief that it exists. Speculative propositions, including simulated reality hypotheses, require their own evidentiary assessment.

